54	14	coach	3	### State Definition and Invariants\n\nDefine the recursive state by the tuple $(L, R, \\Delta)$, where:\n- $[L, R]$ is the contiguous candidate index range known to contain the target anomaly.\n- $\\Delta$ is the net discrepancy/surplus observed in the interval $[L, R]$, defined as:\n  $$\\Delta = Q(L, R) - E(L, R)$$\n  Here, $Q(L, R)$ is the measured query value over the segment, and $E(L, R)$ is the known baseline (expected value when no anomaly is present).\n\n**Primary Invariant:** $\\Delta > 0$ holds strictly on $[L, R]$ throughout the recursion.\n\n---\n\n### Transition and Splitting Relation\n\nTo transition from state $(L, R, \\Delta)$ to a smaller subproblem:\n\n1. **Midpoint Split:**\n   Set $M = \\lfloor (L + R) / 2 \\rfloor$, partitioning $[L, R]$ into $[L, M]$ and $[M + 1, R]$.\n\n2. **Sub-query Evaluation:**\n   Query the left segment $[L, M]$ (or determine it via prefix queries $Q(1, M) - Q(1, L - 1)$) to obtain $Q(L, M)$. Compute its local discrepancy:\n   $$\\Delta_L = Q(L, M) - E(L, M)$$\n\n3. **Discrepancy Conservation:**\n   By the linearity of prefix sums:\n   $$Q(L, R) = Q(L, M) + Q(M + 1, R) \\implies \\Delta = \\Delta_L + \\Delta_R$$\n   Therefore, the right-half discrepancy is implicitly known without an extra query:\n   $$\\Delta_R = \\Delta - \\Delta_L$$\n\n4. **Branching Decision:**\n   - **Case 1 ($\\Delta_L > 0$):** The anomaly (or at least one target point) is guaranteed to lie in the left half. Transition directly to:\n     $$\\text{State} \\leftarrow (L, M, \\Delta_L)$$\n   - **Case 2 ($\\Delta_L = 0$):** The left segment is entirely nominal ($\\Delta_L = 0$). By conservation, $\\Delta_R = \\Delta > 0$. Transition directly to:\n     $$\\text{State} \\leftarrow (M + 1, R, \\Delta)$$\n\n---\n\n### Termination and Output\n\n- **Base Case:** When $L = R$, the interval length is 1. Under the invariant $\\Delta > 0$, the single remaining index $L$ is uniquely identified as the target index.\n- **Query Complexity:** Each leve